Notice that the intersection of the two long diagonals which has been shaded in is also a triangle.  The three outside triangles are each similar to $\triangle ABC$ and to each other.  The others are each similar to $\triangle ABC$ and to $\triangle ABC$ by the $30^\circ$-$60^\circ$-$90^\circ$ rule.

These large triangles each have an areal density equal to the original  side length squared.  But the small inside triangles have an areal density equal to the small side length squared minus the areal density of the large triangle (this is because they are similar and perfectly straight lines).  So the large triangles have one-third the area of the small triangles.

Since the first small triangle has an area of 9, the three remain small triangles have combined area of $27$, and their area is all that is on the shaded side of the large triangle.  The remaining three sides are of two equal length, and therefore of length 6.

Thus the perimeter of the comprised figure is $27+6+6 = \boxed{41}$.
